ARM64 extended BPF atomics backport
==================================

Sources:
- Linux v5.12 atomic UAPI, interpreter and verifier register-definition handling:
  https://github.com/torvalds/linux/tree/v5.12/kernel/bpf
- Linux v5.15.180 check_atomic(), including pointer-leak fixes:
  https://github.com/gregkh/linux/blob/v5.15.180/kernel/bpf/verifier.c
- Linux v6.1.130 ARM64 emit_ll_sc_atomic():
  https://github.com/gregkh/linux/blob/v6.1.130/arch/arm64/net/bpf_jit_comp.c

The ARM64 JIT implements ADD/AND/OR/XOR, fetch variants, XCHG and CMPXCHG
for 32 and 64 bits. Legacy ADD keeps its existing LSE fast path. Extended
operations use upstream LL/SC sequences with release stores and barriers;
they work without LSE. The interpreter implements the same operations.
Other architecture JITs reject extended operations through an explicit
capability hook, rather than silently interpreting them as legacy XADD.
Precision backtracking conservatively marks scalars precise across atomics.

Host check (emitter logic, NOT execution of generated ARM64 instructions):
  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bpf/test_arm64_atomics.py

Build the standalone device test with the Android NDK:
  aarch64-linux-android30-clang -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror \
    tools/testing/selftests/bpf/test_atomics_device.c -o test-atomics

After booting the new kernel, run test-atomics as root. It loads and executes
120 small programs through BPF_PROG_TEST_RUN and verifies three invalid
programs are rejected. With CONFIG_BPF_JIT_ALWAYS_ON this exercises the real
verifier and ARM64 JIT. For an interpreter build, also run with the JIT off.
A permission error does not establish whether atomic support works.

Full kernel compilation and execution on the device remain required. The
host emitter check does not test instruction encodings, the real verifier,
concurrent CPU memory ordering, or the kernel interpreter.
